ds were occupied the  doctors here had to pool their  experience with the disease to find out  which patients were hit particularly  hard  I noticed material this clearly has  something to do with social status  starting with the work you do whether  you are more exposed whether you  couldn't work from home but instead had  to continue working at the supermarket  checkout or on public transport of  course that applies more to low paid  jobs that was a factor and in addition  there is also the risk of more severe  progression if you have the disease  already studies have also shown that  that is clearly related to a certain  social status coronavirus patients stay  in hospital for a long time they start  to talk and then they tell doctor  gellick how badly they're doing in their  everyday lives poverty makes people ill  and that's also the case with Kovac 19  well on that point we can hear from  Miroku yen who is a professor of  international management at the Wharton  School in Philadelphia he's done  research into the effects of poverty on  pandemics so I asked him why coronavirus  seems to be hitting the poorest in  societies hardest let's begin with the  fact that many of them have to continue  working they don't have the luxury that  I have which is of working from home  they have to continue commuting to their  place of work and also maybe they don't  have a car they have to use public  transportation so they're always more  exposed to the virus itself and then in  some countries like in the United States  they have less access to health care and  they also tend to live with many other  people in their homes so for all of  those reasons they are both more exposed  to the virus and then they are not as  well prepared to cope with some of the  likely health consequences and is there  the further negative impact of that  that it actually exacerbates the  pandemic having a split within a santur  society  absolutely so whenever you have duality  whenever you have th